  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrent-Thursday...Strong surface high pressure over the \nAppalachians will migrate toward the mid Atlc coast and combined \nwith lower pressure to the south across the Gulf/FL Straits and \nwestern Atlc will keep a tight pressure gradient over the area. \nEasterly winds will increase to 15-20 mph each day with gusts to \n25-30 mph. Occasional gusts to 35 mph possible at times at the\ncoast. Winds remain elevated at night over the interior around 10\nmph and continued breezy/gusty conditions along the coast. \n\nMoisture increases ahead of weakening low pressure over the Gulf \ncombined with a disturbance aloft to produce ISOLD-SCT (20-50%) to\nlocally numerous (60%) PoPs today. Coverage increases further \ntonight (60-70%) and into Wed-Thu (60-80%) as low-topped showery \nprecip develops across the local coastal waters and at the coast \npushing onshore. A Flood Watch will go into effect later today \nacross much of ECFL. Primary impacts with showers and ISOLD \nlightning storms will be occasional lightning strikes, gusty winds\nlocally 30-45 mph and torrential downpours; widespread amounts up\nto 3-6\"+ (interior) with ISOLD 8-12+\" possible near the coast \nthru late week from repeated rounds. Do not drive through flooded\nroadways if you encounter flooding! \n\nThe Weather Prediction Center has outlooked ECFL in a\nMarginal/Slight Risk (Level 1/2 of 4) for Excessive Rainfall each\nday, with a Moderate Risk on Wednesday (Level 3 of 4) for much of\nthe coast.\n\nExpect seasonable to just below normal highs in the L-U 70s/near 80F \nsouth. Lows mild and generally in the 60s with conditions humid.\n\nA High Surf Advisory goes into effect this evening as winds and \nseas build over the local coastal waters. Rough, pounding surf \nwill promote moderate to locally major beach erosion along the \ncoast. In addition, a HIGH rip current threat will exist. Entering\nthe rough and dangerous surf is strongly discouraged!\n\nFri-Mon...Surface high pressure along the Eastern Seaboard will\nfinally be on a weakening trend into early portions of the\nweekend, as an area of low pressure develops near the northern\nBahamas and lifts northward parallel to the Florida east coast\nFri-Sat. ERLY winds will gradually decrease into the weekend with\ndirection becoming northeast Sat afternoon and further \"backing\" \nto the north/northwest Sat night-Mon surrounding the counter- \nclockwise circulation of the aforementioned low pressure.\n\nWhile fairly high rain chances (60-80%) continue on Fri, they \nwill gradually decrease into the weekend and early next week. The \nFlood Watch will continue into Fri evening. Temperatures for highs\nwill be near to slightly above climo. Low temperatures will be \nabove normal for much of this period. Hazardous coastal/marine \nconditions will continue.",
